## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical integration of image provenance with runtime attestation, ensuring defense in depth for CNF deployments in multi-region clusters.

## Key Concepts
- Image signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SPDX SBOM association and SBOM validation
- In-cluster attestation service generating verifiable tokens
- Admission control enforcing both provenance and attestation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: basic attestation gate
function attest(imageDigest, token, sbom) {
  if (!verifySignature(imageDigest)) return false;
  if (!verifySBOM(sbom)) return false;
  if (!verifyToken(token)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in the KMS?
- How would you simulate attestation failures in a namespace to verify policy behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a comprehensive cross-architecture supply chain security and runtime trust workflow that addresses edge deployment challenges, including offline scenarios and real-time policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-architecture image signing and SBOM provenance tracking
- Runtime attestation with architecture-aware claim verification
- Policy enforcement using OPA Gatekeeper with architecture-specific constraints
- Edge connectivity management and canary-driven rollback mechanisms

## Code Example
```javascript
function isAllowed(image, arch) {
  // Verify Cosign signature for specific architecture
  const signatureValid = verifyCosignSignature(image, arch);
  const sbomPresent = checkSBOMPresence(image, arch);
  const attestationValid = verifyInTotoAttestation(image, arch);

  return signatureValid && sbomPresent && attestationValid;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world traffic shaping at tenant level requires deterministic routing and easy rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Hash-based per-tenant routing
- Namespace ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Canary percentage control and target routing
- Production fallback and validation endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: determine if a request should go to canary
const enabled = getConfig('cnf.canary.enabled');
const pct = getConfig('cnf.canary.percentage');
if (enabled) {
  const h = hash(`${tenantId}${path}`) % 100;
  routeToCanary = h < pct;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement safe rollback if canary metrics drop?
- How to simulate traffic split in tests and monitor drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement end-to-end tracing in a multi-tenant CNF gateway; validates generating and propagating trace context with tenant isolation using OpenTelemetry and OTLP.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry, OTLP exporter
- Trace context propagation (traceparent) and tenant tagging
- Sidecar-based instrumentation and in-cluster collector
- Validation: Jaeger/Tempo dashboards per tenant

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: derive and propagate trace context
const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
const traceId = sha256(tenant + req.path + Date.now()).slice(0, 32);
const spanId  = sha256(tenant + (req.headers['x-request-id'] || Date.now())).slice(0, 16);
req.headers['traceparent'] = `00-${traceId}-${spanId}-01`;
req.headers['X-Tenant-Id'] = tenant;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-tenant trace privacy and access controls?
- What would you monitor if downstream services drop trace headers?
